    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To completely factor the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the greatest common factor (GCF):**\n   First, we identify the greatest common factor of the terms in the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\).\n\n   - The coefficients are 9 and 3. The GCF of 9 and 3 is 3.\n   - The variable part is \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\). The GCF of \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\) is \\(x\\).\n\n   Therefore, the GCF of \\(9x^2\\) and \\(3x\\) is \\(3x\\).\n\n2. **Factor out the GCF:**\n   We factor out \\(3x\\) from each term in the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\).\n\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1)\n   \\]\n\n   This simplifies to:\n\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x + 1)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Check for further factoring:**\n   The factor \\(3x(3x + 1)\\) is in its simplest form because there are no more common factors between \\(3x\\) and \\(3x + 1\\).\n\nTherefore, the completely factored form of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n\\]",
